ates the entire system [Data: Entities (2136), Relationships (2906)]. The 'SISTEMA CENTRALIZADO' often leverages a 'MAINFRAME' as its central server [Data: Relationships (2891)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Critical Vulnerability: Single Point of Failure\",\n      \"explanation\": \"A defining characteristic and significant drawback of the 'SISTEMA CENTRALIZADO' architecture is its inherent 'PONTO ÚNICO DE FALHA' (Single Point of Failure). This means that the entire system's operation is critically dependent on the continuous functioning of the central server. If this central point fails or experiences any interruption, all operations cease, rendering the system completely unavailable [Data: Entities (2136), Relationships (2906)]. This vulnerability is a direct consequence of concentrating all processing and control in one location. The descriptions emphasize that the very nature of this centralized design directly leads to this critical weakness, making the system highly susceptible to disruptions [Data: Relationships (2906)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Historical Adoption by Banks and Universities\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Historically, the 'SISTEMA CENTRALIZADO' architecture was widely adopted by various institutions, notably 'BANCOS' (Banks) and 'UNIVERSIDADES' (Universities). Banks utilized centralized systems for managing their operations and data, likely appreciating the ease of administration and control it offered [Data: Relationships (2899)]. Similarly, universities implemented these systems, particularly during the 1960s and 1970s, for academic and administrative purposes [Data: Relationships (2900)]. This widespread adoption indicates the perceived benefits of centralized systems in terms of management and resource sharing at the time, despite the inherent risks.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Advantages and Disadvantages of Centralized Systems\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The 'SISTEMA CENTRALIZADO' architecture presents a trade-off between administrative simplicity and potential scalability and reliability issues. Advantages include centralized administration and control, making management potentially simpler [Data: Relationships (2918)]. Security and data integrity can also be facilitated due to the centralization, simplifying backup procedures [Data: Relationships (2919)]. However, the disadvantages are significant. The system suffers from low scalability, with performance degradation as the number of simultaneous users increases [Data: Relationships (2920)]. Furthermore, the high cost of central infrastructure and the critical vulnerability of a single point of failure are major drawbacks [Data: Relationships (2918, 2920)]. LIN_001, LIN_002, and LIN_003 provide detailed insights into these aspects, covering administration, security, and performance [Data: Relationships (2918, 2919, 2920)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Role of Communication Lines in Centralized Systems\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The 'LINHA DE COMUNICAÇÃO' (Communication Line) is an indispensable component of the 'SISTEMA CENTRALIZADO' architecture. It acts as the physical or logical medium that connects the central server to all interactive terminals [Data: Entities (2135)]. This line is fundamental for enabling the flow of data and information, ensuring that terminals can communicate with the central unit and receive the necessary resources and functionalities. Without an effective communication line, the centralized system cannot function, as the terminals would be unable to interact with the server [Data: Relationships (2904)]. The efficiency and reliability of this communication line directly impact the overall performance and user experience of the centralized system.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"rating\": 7.5,\n  \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is high due to the critical single point of failure inherent in centralized systems, which can lead to widespread disruption for dependent o